window.docContents[107].resolve({"/Tier-1___-statements/Statement-files-_LPAR_T_RPAR_/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.SignalPqxdh.Model/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___SignalPqxdh___Model":{"contents":"Paper › LibsignalBinary/Specifications/SignalPqxdh › Model.leanTier 1 · statement file\n\nTier 1 · statement file used by the computational proof and symbolic proof 13 headline theorems depend on it 947 lines trust banner B1, trusted: the statement source ↗Read in full. A statement file: its definitions say what the headline theorems claim (games, adversaries, bounds). Proofs are cut after := and shown as ⋯; Lean checks them.\n\nDeclarations on this page (81)abbrev Bytesstructure DRKeystructure MessageKeysdef TAG_EC_DJBdef TAG_KYBER_1024def F_X25519def INFO_PQXDHdef INFO_RATCHETdef INFO_MSG_KEYSdef encodeECdef kyberPkLendef kyberCtLendef encodeKEMstructure Primitivesdef buildSecretsdef pbVarintdef pbBytesFielddef firstMsgADstructure PreKeyBundleInputstructure HandshakeHdrstructure PreKeyMsgstructure InitiateKeysInputstructure InitiateInputstructure AcceptKeysInputstructure AcceptInputdef dhOptNonZerodef Model.initiateKeysdef Model.acceptKeysdef Model.initiatedef Model.acceptdef montLiftEddef xeddsaVerifyRefdef scalarIsInRangedef isCanonicalRefdef fitBytesReftheorem fitBytesRef_lengthdef clampScalar25519def sha256InitStatedef sha512InitStatedef encodeBitLen64BEdef encodeBitLen128BEdef sha256Paddef sha512Paddef chunkBlocksdef sha256Stepdef sha512Stepdef sha256Refdef sha512Refdef xorBytesdef hmacSha256Refdef hkdfExtractRefdef hkdfExpand96Refdef hkdfDerive96Refdef rootCreateChainRefdef chainKdfSeedRefdef chainKdfNextRefdef deriveMsgKeysRefdef pkcs7Pad16def pkcs7Unpad16def toBlocks16def fromBlocks16def cbcEncBlocksWithdef cbcDecBlocksWithabbrev cbcEncBlocksabbrev cbcDecBlocksdef aes256CbcEncRefdef aes256CbcDecRefdef firstMsgHdrLendef aeadMacInputdef aes256CbcHmacEncRefdef aes256CbcHmacDecRefdef onCurveKeydef isValidDhTripleRefstructure WellFormedKemδstructure WellFormedSpqrstructure WellFormedDhstructure WellFormedSigstructure WellFormedKdfstructure WellFormedAeadstructure WellFormedPrimitivesδdef PqxdhCorrect\n\n\n\nPure Byte-Level Specification of the Signal PQXDH Handshake and First MessageTrusted: the statement. Machine-free functional model of the PQXDH key agreement (Model.initiateKeys, Model.acceptKeys) and first-message key schedule (Model.initiate, Model.accept) of libsignal-protocol v0.105.4 over byte lists (Bytes := List UInt8), parameterized by Primitives, together with reference cryptographic constructions, the structural and algebraic well-formedness bundle WellFormedPrimitivesδ (without perfect KEM decapsulation correctness, DEV-PQ21), and functional correctness PqxdhCorrect. See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md.\n\n\n\nabbrev BytesL32–33 ↗#Byte string representation used across SignalPqxdh.\n\nstructure DRKeyL35–40 ↗#96-byte PQXDH handshake output (HandshakeKeys, pqxdh.rs:51-68).\n\nstructure MessageKeysL42–47 ↗#80-byte per-message keys (MessageKeys, ratchet/keys.rs:91-97).\n\ndef TAG_EC_DJBL49–50 ↗#Key-type tag byte 0x05 of serialized Curve25519 (DJB) public keys in libsignal-core.\n\ndef TAG_KYBER_1024L51–52 ↗#Key-type tag byte 0x08 of serialized Kyber1024 public keys in libsignal-protocol.\n\ndef F_X25519L54–55 ↗#32-byte 0xFF domain separator prefix for X25519 in X3DH/PQXDH (pqxdh.rs:200, 337).\n\ndef INFO_PQXDHL57–59 ↗#46-byte ASCII HKDF info string (pqxdh.rs:28-30, 74, 82).\n\ndef INFO_RATCHETL61–63 ↗#14-byte ASCII HKDF info string (ratchet/keys.rs:207).\n\ndef INFO_MSG_KEYSL65–67 ↗#18-byte ASCII HKDF info string (ratchet/keys.rs:108).\n\ndef encodeECL69–70 ↗#Serializes a 32-byte X25519 public key into 33 bytes: 0x05 ‖ pk.\n\ndef kyberPkLenL72–73 ↗#Byte length of a Kyber1024 public key (rust/protocol/src/kem.rs).\n\ndef kyberCtLenL75–76 ↗#Byte length of a Kyber1024 ciphertext (rust/protocol/src/kem.rs).\n\ndef encodeKEML78–79 ↗#Serializes a kyberPkLen-byte Kyber1024 public key into 1569 bytes: 0x08 ‖ pk.\n\nstructure PrimitivesL81–165 ↗#The abstract cryptographic primitives that parameterize the Model.* protocol functions: one field per operation used by the handshake and the 5-step first-message key schedule, plus the block functions and curve arithmetic used by the reference constructions below.\n\ndef buildSecretsL167–171 ↗#Assembles the 160-byte (no OPK) or 192-byte (with OPK) secrets buffer (pqxdh.rs:202-231).\n\ndef pbVarintL173–181 ↗#Protobuf base-128 varint (prost::encoding::encode_varint): 7 bits per byte, least significant group first, high bit set on every byte but the last.\n\ndef pbBytesFieldL183–185 ↗#Protobuf bytes field tag (wire type 2): key byte 8·tag + 2, length varint, contents.\n\ndef firstMsgADL187–197 ↗#First-message associated data authenticated by the 8-byte MAC in SignalMessage::compute_mac (protocol.rs:214-235): ser(ikA) ‖ ser(ikB), then the version byte 0x44 and the prost encoding of the inner SignalMessage header fields: ratchet_key = 1 (encodeEC ephAPk), counter = 2 and previous_counter = 3 (both 0 for the first message) and, if non-empty, pq_ratchet = 5. The first firstMsgHdrLen bytes are the fields that precede the ciphertext; aeadMacInput inserts the ciphertext field (ciphertext = 4) there, so the MAC input has prost's tag order 1, 2, 3, 4, 5. addresses = 6 is omitted (DEV-PQ31).\n\nstructure PreKeyBundleInputL199–210 ↗#Bob's prekey bundle as received on the wire by Alice (PreKeyBundle). Only the single wire identity public key ikB (32B) exists on the wire.\n\nstructure HandshakeHdrL212–221 ↗#Handshake-only wire header output by Model.initiateKeys (also the header type used by the PQXDH games in Computational/Specifications/Pqxdh/Game.lean).\n\nstructure PreKeyMsgL223–229 ↗#Full first-message wire packet output by Model.initiate (PreKeySignalMessage).\n\nstructure InitiateKeysInputL231–240 ↗#Alice's inputs to handshake-only Model.initiateKeys (and full Model.initiate). Only Alice's single wire identity key (ikASk, ikAPk) is present.\n\nstructure InitiateInputL242–247 ↗#Alice's inputs to full first-message Model.initiate (process_prekey_bundle + first send).\n\nstructure AcceptKeysInputL249–261 ↗#Bob's inputs to handshake-only Model.acceptKeys (<Pqxdh as Handshake>::accept).\n\nstructure AcceptInputL263–267 ↗#Bob's inputs to full first-message Model.accept (process_prekey + message_decrypt_prekey).\n\ndef dhOptNonZeroL269–274 ↗#Check that an optional 32-byte X25519 shared secret (dh4?) is non-zero when present (PrivateKey::calculate_agreement in rust/core/src/curve.rs).\n\ndef Model.initiateKeysL276–311 ↗#Core PQXDH handshake initiation (<Pqxdh as Handshake>::initiate / pqxdh_initiate, pqxdh.rs:97-205, 194-237): verifies bundle signatures against the single wire key bundle.ikB, encapsulates against pqspkB, computes the 3 or 4 X25519 legs, and derives the 96-byte DRKey.\n\ndef Model.acceptKeysL313–344 ↗#Core PQXDH handshake acceptance (<Pqxdh as Handshake>::accept / pqxdh_accept, pqxdh.rs:208-291, 326-376, with mark_kyber_pre_key_used at pqxdh.rs:246-254 and storage/inmem.rs:247-265): checks is_canonical(ekAPk), computes 3 or 4 DH legs, decapsulates kemCt, and derives DRKey.\n\ndef Model.initiateL346–374 ↗#Faithful first-message initiation (5-step key schedule; session.rs:200-285, ratchet.rs:67-112, triple_ratchet.rs:86-135): runs Model.initiateKeys to obtain drKey = (rk0, ck0, pqr0), then executes rootCreateChain(rk0, DH(ephA, spkB)) → chainKdf → spqrInitSend → deriveMsgKeys → aeadEnc.\n\ndef Model.acceptL376–399 ↗#Faithful first-message acceptance (5-step key schedule; session.rs:39-198, ratchet.rs:118-172, triple_ratchet.rs:215-298, session_management.rs:207-285): runs Model.acceptKeys to obtain drKey = (rk0, ck0, pqr0), then executes rootCreateChain(rk0, DH(spkB, ephA)) → chainKdf → spqrInitRecv → deriveMsgKeys → aeadDec.\n\ndef montLiftEdL401–416 ↗#Birational lift from a 32-byte little-endian Montgomery u-coordinate (RFC 7748 §5 bit-255 masked, reduced mod p = 2^255 - 19) and low sign bit sign &&& 1 to the affine twisted Edwards curve (y = (u - 1) / (u + 1) mod p, rejecting u = p - 1 where u + 1 ≡ 0 and u on the quadratic twist (u³ + 486662 u² + u non-square, detected by recoverXEven)). Reducing u ≥ p mod p follows libsignal / curve25519-dalek (MontgomeryPoint::to_edwards), not Perrin's XEdDSA §2.5 (PublicKey::is_canonical checks scalarIsInRange separately).\n\ndef xeddsaVerifyRefL418–439 ↗#Functional reference specification of Signal's XEdDSA signature verification (curve25519::verify_signature_for_multipart_message, rust/core/src/curve/curve25519.rs:119-160). The range check is on sig[63] &&& 0x60 because the sign bit (bit 7) is extracted and cleared before (s[31] & 0xE0) == 0 is tested.\n\ndef scalarIsInRangeL441–450 ↗#Constant-time little-endian range check scalar_is_in_range from rust/core/src/curve.rs:176-186: returns true iff bit 7 of byte 31 is 0 ((pk.getD 31 0 &&& 0x80) == 0) and the little-endian 255-bit integer pk is strictly less than 2^255 - 19, i.e., not pk[0] >= 0xED ∧ pk[1..31] == [0xFF; 30] ∧ (pk[31] & 0x7F) == 0x7F.\n\ndef isCanonicalRefL452–460 ↗#Functional reference specification of <libsignal_core::curve::PublicKey>::is_canonical (rust/core/src/curve.rs:156-191).\n\ndef fitBytesRefL462–464 ↗#Pad or truncate a Bytes list to n bytes (reference helper).\n\ntheorem fitBytesRef_lengthL466–469 ↗#\n\ndef clampScalar25519L471–478 ↗#RFC 7748 32-byte scalar clamping (x25519_dalek::StaticSecret::from / clamp_integer): clears the low 3 bits of byte 0 (&&& 0xF8), clears bit 7 and sets bit 6 of byte 31 ((b &&& 0x7F) ||| 0x40), and preserves bytes 1..30.\n\ndef sha256InitStateL480–485 ↗#FIPS 180-4 §5.3.3 SHA-256 initial 32-byte chaining state (H^(0)).\n\ndef sha512InitStateL487–496 ↗#FIPS 180-4 §5.3.5 SHA-512 initial 64-byte chaining state (H^(0)).\n\ndef encodeBitLen64BEL498–508 ↗#Big-endian 64-bit bit-length encoding (8 * byteLen).\n\ndef encodeBitLen128BEL510–512 ↗#Big-endian 128-bit bit-length encoding (8 * byteLen) for SHA-512.\n\ndef sha256PadL514–518 ↗#FIPS 180-4 §5.1.1 SHA-256 message padding to a multiple of 64 bytes.\n\ndef sha512PadL520–524 ↗#FIPS 180-4 §5.1.2 SHA-512 message padding to a multiple of 128 bytes.\n\ndef chunkBlocksL526–529 ↗#Splits bs into fixed-size chunks of blockSize bytes.\n\ndef sha256StepL531–533 ↗#Single 64-byte block SHA-256 compression step (normalizing state to 32 bytes).\n\ndef sha512StepL535–537 ↗#Single 128-byte block SHA-512 compression step (normalizing state to 64 bytes).\n\ndef sha256RefL539–542 ↗#Merkle-Damgård SHA-256 reference construction over P.sha256Compress (sha2::Sha256).\n\ndef sha512RefL544–547 ↗#Merkle-Damgård SHA-512 reference construction over P.sha512Compress (sha2::Sha512).\n\ndef xorBytesL549–551 ↗#Bytewise XOR of two byte lists (List.zipWith (· ^^^ ·)).\n\ndef hmacSha256RefL553–559 ↗#RFC 2104 HMAC-SHA256 reference construction built from sha256Ref P.\n\ndef hkdfExtractRefL561–566 ↗#RFC 5869 HKDF-Extract(salt?, ikm) using hmacSha256Ref P (hkdf::Hkdf::<Sha256>::new).\n\ndef hkdfExpand96RefL568–573 ↗#RFC 5869 HKDF-Expand(prk, info, 96) into three 32-byte blocks (T(1), T(2), T(3)).\n\ndef hkdfDerive96RefL575–578 ↗#Reference PQXDH handshake KDF (pqxdh.rs:70-92): HKDF-SHA256(salt = None, ikm = secrets).expand(INFO_PQXDH, 96).\n\ndef rootCreateChainRefL580–586 ↗#Reference Epoch-1 Root KDF (RootKey::create_chain, ratchet/keys.rs:199-218): HKDF-SHA256(salt = Some rk0, ikm = dhRatchet).expand(INFO_RATCHET, 64).\n\ndef chainKdfSeedRefL588–591 ↗#Reference Epoch-1 Chain KDF seed (ChainKey::message_keys, ratchet/keys.rs:172-182): HMAC-SHA256(key = ck, [0x01]).\n\ndef chainKdfNextRefL593–596 ↗#Reference Epoch-1 Chain KDF step (ChainKey::next_chain_key, ratchet/keys.rs:165-170): HMAC-SHA256(key = ck, [0x02]).\n\ndef deriveMsgKeysRefL598–605 ↗#Reference MessageKeys::derive_keys (ratchet/keys.rs:100-118): HKDF-SHA256(salt = spqrSalt?, ikm = mkSeed).expand(INFO_MSG_KEYS, 80) split 32 / 32 / 16.\n\ndef pkcs7Pad16L607–611 ↗#PKCS#7 padding to a 16-byte block boundary (block_padding::Pkcs7, crypto.rs:14-35).\n\ndef pkcs7Unpad16L613–623 ↗#PKCS#7 unpadding on 16-byte blocks (block_padding::Pkcs7, crypto.rs:37-61).\n\ndef toBlocks16L625–634 ↗#Splits a byte list into 16-byte blocks.\n\ndef fromBlocks16L636–638 ↗#Concatenates 16-byte blocks back into a flat byte list.\n\ndef cbcEncBlocksWithL640–645 ↗#Encrypts 16-byte blocks in AES-256-CBC mode using an AES block encryptor.\n\ndef cbcDecBlocksWithL647–652 ↗#Decrypts 16-byte blocks in AES-256-CBC mode using an AES block decryptor.\n\nabbrev cbcEncBlocksL654–656 ↗#Encrypts 16-byte blocks in AES-256-CBC mode using P.aesEncBlock.\n\nabbrev cbcDecBlocksL658–660 ↗#Decrypts 16-byte blocks in AES-256-CBC mode using P.aesDecBlock.\n\ndef aes256CbcEncRefL662–665 ↗#Reference AES-256-CBC encryption with PKCS#7 padding (libsignal_protocol::crypto::aes_256_cbc_encrypt, crypto.rs:14-35, backed by P.aesEncBlock).\n\ndef aes256CbcDecRefL667–673 ↗#Reference AES-256-CBC decryption with PKCS#7 unpadding (libsignal_protocol::crypto::aes_256_cbc_decrypt, crypto.rs:37-61, backed by P.aesDecBlock).\n\ndef firstMsgHdrLenL675–679 ↗#Length of the part of firstMsgAD before pq_ratchet when the three EC keys have 32 bytes: ser(ikA) and ser(ikB) (33 each), 0x44 (1), ratchet_key (0x0a 0x21 and 33 bytes), counter and previous_counter (0x10 0x00 0x18 0x00). Model.acceptKeys and Model.accept reject received EC keys of any other length.\n\ndef aeadMacInputL681–686 ↗#HMAC input of the first-message AEAD: the prost-serialized SignalMessage that compute_mac authenticates (DEV-PQ31). The ciphertext, as the length-delimited field ciphertext = 4 (0x22 ‖ varint |ct| ‖ ct), is placed after the first firstMsgHdrLen bytes of ad (ser(ikA) ‖ ser(ikB) ‖ 0x44 ‖ fields 1-3) and before the rest of ad (field 5 if present).\n\ndef aes256CbcHmacEncRefL688–694 ↗#Reference first-message AEAD encryption (session_cipher.rs:125-175, protocol.rs:78-125): AES-256-CBC-PKCS#7 ciphertext followed by the 8-byte truncated HMAC-SHA256(macKey, aeadMacInput ad ct) tag.\n\ndef aes256CbcHmacDecRefL696–710 ↗#Reference first-message AEAD decryption (session_cipher.rs:290-360, protocol.rs:236-250): verifies the 8-byte trailing HMAC-SHA256(macKey, aeadMacInput ad ct) tag and runs aes256CbcDecRef.\n\ndef onCurveKeyL712–719 ↗#pk is an X25519 input on which scalar multiplication is determined by the scalar's residue modulo ±ℓ: either a canonical key (isCanonicalRef: 32 bytes, high bit clear, u < p, montToEd succeeds and torsion-free, hence a curve point of order exactly ℓ) or a point whose pow8 projection agrees with that of an honest base-point output (so, by x25519_pow8_congr, X25519 on pk behaves like X25519 on a prime-subgroup curve point). Twist points are excluded: they satisfy neither disjunct.\n\ndef isValidDhTripleRefL721–732 ↗#Reference DDH predicate, gated on onCurveKey: (X, Y, Z) is a DH triple iff both X and Y satisfy onCurveKey P and some scalar a with X = x25519Base a has pow8 Z = pow8 (x25519 a Y), or the same holds with X and Y swapped. Requiring onCurveKey P X and onCurveKey P Y rejects quadratic-twist and small-order points in either key slot, so the DDH oracle in GapDH, GapDhWithSign and GapSigEufCma never answers cross-curve queries on 8k₀ mod ℓ' (on onCurveKey inputs, x25519_congr_of_base_eq makes x25519 a Y independent of the choice of scalar a with x25519Base a = X). Law isValidDhTriple_eq_ref pins P.isValidDhTriple to this predicate.\n\nstructure WellFormedKemδL734–753 ↗#Kyber-1024 structural well-formedness laws on P : Primitives without perfect decapsulation correctness (kem_corr, DEV-PQ21): encapsulation/decapsulation totality on well-sized inputs and output byte lengths.\n\nstructure WellFormedSpqrL755–772 ↗#SPQR initial-step well-formedness laws on P : Primitives: initiator totality, initiator/responder salt agreement, and received salt length bound.\n\nstructure WellFormedDhL774–866 ↗#RFC 7748 X25519, canonicality, cofactor-8 subgroup projection (pow8), and DDH-oracle consistency laws on P : Primitives.Satisfiability requirement. Every law below is required to hold for real RFC 7748 X25519 on ALL 32-byte inputs, including twist points and small-order points (an adversary may supply any 32 bytes as a public key). A real-X25519 witness: let pow8 Q := [c]Q where c is the CRT idempotent with c ≡ 1 (mod ℓ), c ≡ 0 (mod 8) on the curve and c' ≡ 1 (mod ℓ'), c' ≡ 0 (mod 4) on the twist, all torsion points mapping to 0^32 (encoded as a 32-byte u-coordinate); let isValidDhTriple X Y Z hold iff isValidDhTripleRef X Y Z (law isValidDhTriple_eq_ref). canon_x25519 and ddh_consistent_left carry hypotheses (canonical input, onCurveKey) because their unrestricted forms do not hold for this witness (and for real X25519 under any choice of pow8/isValidDhTriple).\n\nstructure WellFormedSigL868–881 ↗#XEdDSA signing/verification, single-key public-key identity (sigPkGen = x25519Base), SHA-512 reference link, and Curve25519 scalar and compressed-point byte lengths on P : Primitives.\n\nstructure WellFormedKdfL883–891 ↗#SHA-256 compression block length and reference-construction equations for the handshake, Epoch-1 root, chain, and message-key HKDF/HMAC derivations on P : Primitives.\n\nstructure WellFormedAeadL893–902 ↗#AES-256 block-cipher lengths/roundtrip and first-message CBC-HMAC AEAD reference links and decryption roundtrip on P : Primitives.\n\nstructure WellFormedPrimitivesδL904–913 ↗#Structural and algebraic well-formedness of P : Primitives without the perfect KEM decapsulation correctness law kem_corr (DEV-PQ21), assembled from the six component bundles WellFormedKemδ, WellFormedSpqr, WellFormedDh, WellFormedSig, WellFormedKdf, and WellFormedAead. KEM correctness is assumed separately via KemDeltaCorrect P δ (Computational/Specifications/Assumptions/KemCorrect.lean), while proof-side WellFormedPrimitives (LibsignalBinary/Proofs/SpecLemmas/SignalPqxdh/ModelProofDefs.lean) extends WellFormedPrimitivesδ with kem_corr.\n\ndef PqxdhCorrectL915–945 ↗#Functional correctness of PQXDH: for every honest run (authentic bundle and signatures, trusted peers, no replay, KEM and SPQR succeed), Model.initiateKeys/Model.acceptKeys and Model.initiate/Model.accept all succeed with identical (drKey, rk1, ck1_1) and Bob recovers Alice's plaintext. The correctness conjunct of the PQXDH headline statement (Computational/Specifications/Pqxdh/Security/Headline.lean).\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"LibsignalBinary/Specifications/SignalPqxdh/Model.lean","id":"/Tier-1___-statements/Statement-files-_LPAR_T_RPAR_/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.SignalPqxdh.Model/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___SignalPqxdh___Model"}});